  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Fedya argues against opening the cooler door for fear of prison, when the inspector's wife suddenly arrives looking for her husband. Chef Barinov lies that he hasn't seen him and orders Fedya to keep an eye on her.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Не открывайте эту дверь сейчас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Нас всех за это засудят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "А мне в тюрьму нельзя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Открывайте, он же там замёрзнет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Здравствуйте, сюда заходил мой муж?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Он хотел поговорить с шефом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, он сюда не заходил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Шеф, а если она запаникует?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Она же сразу полицию вызовет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Надо тихо проследить за ней.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Федя, его жена на тебе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А с ним попробуем договориться.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Не открывайте эту дверь сейчас.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not open this door right now.' The meaning hinges on 'not' working together with 'now' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Нас всех за это засудят.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They will prosecute all of us for this.' Notice how Russian expresses 'us' and 'they will prosecute' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "А мне в тюрьму нельзя.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And I cannot go to prison.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'and' before landing on 'one must not'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Открывайте, он же там замёрзнет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Open up, he will freeze in there.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'open' and 'he' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Здравствуйте, сюда заходил мой муж?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, did my husband stop by here?' The meaning hinges on 'hello' working together with 'husband' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Он хотел поговорить с шефом.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He wanted to speak with the chef.' the guest anchors the line on 'he', while 'with' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нет, он сюда не заходил.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'No, he did not come in here.' The meaning hinges on 'no' working together with 'went in' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Шеф, а если она запаникует?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Chef, what if she panics?' Notice how Russian expresses 'Chef Barinov' and 'she will panic' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "fedya",
      "t": "Она же сразу полицию вызовет.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She will call the police right away.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'she' and finishing on 'will call'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Надо тихо проследить за ней.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We need to quietly keep an eye on her.' Notice how Russian expresses 'need' and 'her' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Федя, его жена на тебе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Fedya, his wife is your responsibility.' Chef Barinov addresses the listener with the informal singular 'you' alongside 'to you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А с ним попробуем договориться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And we will try to reach an agreement with him.' Chef Barinov brings out the contrast between 'and' and 'with' in a few short words. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
